---
/** A random deck generator, and the walkthrough of how it is built. The
 * code the page runs is the code the page shows: one module, split at
 * its section comments so each step sits next to its prose. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import source from "../../fun/random-deck.js?raw";
import { ELEMENTS } from "../../fun/random-deck.js";

/** The module's sections, in order, by their "// ── N." comments. */
const sections = source.split(/^(?=\/\/ ── )/m).map((chunk) => chunk.trim());
const step = (n: number) => sections.find((s) => s.startsWith(`// ── ${n}.`)) ?? "";
const sourceUrl = "https://github.com/sadkinglabs/kairos-archive/blob/main/src/fun/random-deck.js";
---
<Base title="Random deck" canonical="/fun/random-deck" description="Pick elements and get a random Sorcery deck drawn to the copy rules, from the Kairos Archive query API and image CDN. The code is on the page, step by step.">
  <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/fun">Fun Zone</a></p>
  <h1>Random deck</h1>
  <p class="intro">Choose the elements you want to play and deal a deck: one avatar, a spellbook of sixty spells and an atlas of thirty sites, no card above what its rarity allows. It is drawn in your browser from the <a href="/docs/query">query API</a>, and every picture comes from the <a href="/docs/images">image CDN</a>. The whole program is about a hundred lines, and it is all on this page.</p>

  <form id="deck-form" class="deck-form" aria-label="Choose elements">
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Elements</legend>
      <div class="chips">
        {ELEMENTS.map((e) => <label><input type="checkbox" name="element" value={e} checked={e === "Fire" || e === "Water"} /> {e}</label>)}
      </div>
    </fieldset>
    <p class="muted small">Cards with no element are always in the pool. A card with two elements needs both chosen.</p>
    <button type="submit">Deal a deck</button>
    <p id="deck-status" class="muted" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </form>
  <section id="deck" class="deck" aria-live="polite"></section>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>
  <p>Seven small parts, in the order they run. The code under each heading is the code this page is running, read from the same file.</p>

  <h3>0. The rules</h3>
  <p>Everything the deck must obey, in one place. Four copies of an Ordinary, three of an Exceptional, two of an Elite, one of a Unique; sixty in the spellbook, thirty in the atlas; and two avatars a random deck should not hand you.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(0)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · the rules" />

  <h3>1. Ask for the pool</h3>
  <p>One query per zone, written in the <a href="/syntax">search syntax</a> this site uses. <code>cat:spell</code> is every spell, which in Sorcery means minions, magics, auras and artifacts; <code>t:site</code> is every site; <code>t:avatar</code> every avatar. <code>e:air,fire</code> reads "has Air or has Fire", <code>e:none</code> is a card with no element at all, and <code>-!"Duplicator"</code> drops one exact name. Try any of these in the search box first; the API answers exactly what the site would show.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(1)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · the queries" />

  <h3>2. Fetch every page</h3>
  <p>The API answers a page at a time, up to two hundred cards, and each answer says whether there is more. The loop asks until there is not. There are fewer than a thousand spells, so a full pool is at most five requests.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(2)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · paging" />

  <h3>3. Keep the elements honest</h3>
  <p><code>e:air,fire</code> admits a card that has Air and Water, because it has Air. A deck of Air and Fire should not hold it. The API could be asked for every exact combination, but a one-line filter in the browser reads better: keep a card only when every element it has was chosen. A card with no element always passes.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(3)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · the filter" />

  <h3>4. Draw</h3>
  <p>Shuffle the pool, then walk it: for each card take a random number of copies, never more than its rarity allows and never more than the zone still needs, and stop when the zone is full. A pool too small to fill a zone gives a short zone, which the list says plainly, rather than a fifth copy.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(4)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · the draw" />

  <h3>5. A deck</h3>
  <p>Three pools, loaded once per choice of elements, then a deal that touches no network at all. That is why "Deal a deck" is instant the second time: the API was asked three times, and the shuffling is yours. The query API allows sixty requests a minute from one address; a tool that fetched the pool for every click would spend that in twenty clicks.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(5)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · pools and a deal" />

  <h3>6. Write it down</h3>
  <p>The deck as text, the way a deck list is usually shared: the avatar, then each zone grouped by card type with the count in front of each name.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(6)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · the list" />

  <h3>7. Show it</h3>
  <p>Every card record the API returns carries <code>image_urls</code> for its default printing, four sizes at permanent addresses on the CDN, and <code>kairos_url</code>, its page on this site. A tile is an image and a link. The images are © Erik's Curiosa, served with credit; anything built on them should say so too. The rest is the form: the chosen elements go in the address bar so a choice can be shared.</p>
  <CodeExample code={step(7)} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js · tiles and the form" />

  <h2>Make it yours</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Narrow the pool with the query: <code>s:alpha</code> for one set, <code>cost&lt;=3</code> for a cheap deck, <code>-is:errata</code> to skip cards whose text has changed, <code>k:spellcaster</code> for a theme.</li>
    <li>Shape the curve: draw sites and spells by cost bands instead of one pass.</li>
    <li>Run it anywhere: the module has no browser code above step 7, so the same functions work in Node, a bot or a spreadsheet script. Send a <code>User-Agent</code> that names your tool when it is not a browser; the <a href="/usage">usage terms</a> ask for it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>All of it</h2>
  <p>The file this page runs, unabridged: <a href={sourceUrl}>random-deck.js on GitHub</a>.</p>
  <CodeExample code={source} lang="javascript" title="random-deck.js" />
</Base>

<script>
  import "../../fun/random-deck.js";
</script>

<style>
  .deck-form { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: var(--card); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .deck-form fieldset { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .deck-form legend { font-weight: 600; }
  .deck-form .chips { margin: .5rem 0 .75rem; font-size: 1rem; }
  .deck-form .small { font-size: .85rem; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
  .deck-form #deck-status { margin: .75rem 0 0; }
  /* The deck is built at runtime, so its elements carry no scope attribute: global selectors under the scoped container. */
  .deck :global(h3) { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .deck :global(.grid) { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .deck :global(.rules) { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .deck :global(.deck-text) { margin-top: 1rem; }
</style>
